          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night.",
          "answer": "Nothing in your description matched the emergency patterns I checked. That is not the same as saying it is safe. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations, so a symptom it does not list can still be serious. If your son's breathing is severe, getting worse, or new and unexplained, seek medical advice promptly.\n\nThe asthma overview (*Asthma*, general clinical reference) notes that wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough often get worse at night or in the early morning. The asthma guideline (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) says that asthma is confirmed with a symptom pattern plus breathing tests, usually spirometry. It also says a normal test on a good day does not rule asthma out. Night-time wheeze is one of the patterns that guideline describes, but these sources do not tell me what is causing your son's wheeze.\n\nIt would be reasonable to take him to a clinician, such as his doctor or pediatrician, to describe when the wheeze happens, what seems to bring it on, and whether he has other symptoms. The sources I have do not cover children's breathing specifically, so a clinician who knows him is the right person to assess it.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
