          "type": "turn",
          "question": "He is four.",
          "answer": "Your son's age matters, and I checked the description again. The check found no emergency pattern, but it only covers a fixed list of presentations, so that is not a clearance.\n\nThe \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance (*Emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance*) lists signs that need emergency help right away. For a child, these include fast breathing, skin pulling in between or under the ribs, grunting, flaring nostrils, or being too breathless to feed or talk. It also lists blue or gray lips or face, and no improvement after reliever treatment. If you see any of these, call emergency services now.\n\nThe asthma overview (*Asthma*, general clinical reference) notes that night-time wheeze and cough are common patterns. But these sources do not cover breathing problems in young children, and they do not tell me what is causing your son's wheeze. A doctor or pediatrician who knows him should assess it, and it is worth telling them about the night-time pattern and his age.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
